prompt:
Read web/src/home.ts (`missionsToday`, `InFlight` with objective 069's `conflicts` and `conflictText`) and web/src/missions.ts (a task's `objective`, `created_at_ms`, `stages`). This objective comes after 069 and builds on its fields.

The problem. When a task is cancelled and its objective is worked again, the new task starts its count from nothing. On the live `/api/missions` (read-only, 2026-11-12):
- objective 19 was sent back as a conflict twice under T88, and twice more under T127;
- objective 23 three times under T113, and once under T167;
- objective 28 once each under T175, T202 and T203, so no single attempt was ever sent back twice.
Objective 069 counts only the task's own verdicts, so a third conflict round on one objective reads as the first, and 069's line never shows for objective 28.

Required behaviour.
(a) Every in-flight row of `missionsToday` carries `earlierConflicts`: the number of `conflict` gate verdicts on the mission's other tasks for the same objective that were assigned before it. It is 0 for a task with no objective. Another mission's objective with the same number is not the same objective.
(b) `conflictText` counts the task's own `conflicts` and `earlierConflicts` together:
- from two in all, it reads "sent back <n> times as a conflict", followed by ", <e> on earlier attempts" when <e> is above zero;
- under two, it is null.
069's rows without earlier attempts read as they do.
(c) Home shows the text, as 069 does.

Acceptance: rsi/scenarios/073.sh. Records are folded by `spawn_control_refold()`, and `spawn serve` answers `GET /api/missions`. Backlog missions 1 and 2. In mission 1:
- objective 1: T1 one conflict, then cancelled; T2 one conflict, then cancelled; T3 one conflict, now sent back;
- objective 2: T4 two conflicts, then cancelled; T5 in work with none;
- objective 3: T6 two conflicts, now sent back;
- objective 4: T7 rejected once, then cancelled; T8 one conflict, now sent back.
In mission 2, objective 3's T9 had two conflicts and was cancelled.
- (1) Under node, the in-flight rows are:
  - T3 {conflicts 1, earlierConflicts 2, "sent back 3 times as a conflict, 2 on earlier attempts"};
  - T5 {0, 2, "sent back 2 times as a conflict, 2 on earlier attempts"};
  - T6 {2, 0, "sent back 2 times as a conflict"};
  - T8 {1, 0, null}.
It fails with 1 on ecb177ab: the rows carry no conflict counts at all, because 069 is not built there.

The product reason. Conflict send-backs are the fleet's largest rework, and most of them come from owner pushes. An objective that keeps colliding across attempts is exactly what the owner should see. Counting from zero at each attempt hides the worst cases.

Rules: never edit the protected paths, which the gate rejects: rsi/, scripts/ (scripts/rsi/, scripts/blackbox/, scripts/e2e.sh among them), deploy/, all of web/ except web/src/, web/test/ and web/index.html, rust-toolchain files and .cargo/. The gate also rejects fewer runnable tests than the base, any lint relaxation and any build-time code. The gate builds offline from the cargo caches already on the host. Keep every existing check green (069's included) and add web/test tests of your own for (a) to (c): earlier attempts with and without conflicts, a later task of the same objective not counted, another mission's objective not counted, a task with no objective.
